            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book successfully unifies the diverse uses of \\\"butter\\\" into a single, cohesive symbolic framework, whereas the common interpretation must divide the occurrences into disconnected idioms (prosperity, subsistence, flattery, cause-and-effect). By combining Proverbs 30:33 (butter as milk worked/churned) with Isaiah 7:15 (eating butter in order to refuse evil and choose good), the book elegantly derives \\\"the word worked from milk into sound judgment.\\\" This naturally parallels the broader biblical distinction between milk (basic teaching) and solid food for those with exercised discernment (Hebrews 5). The secondary traits—washing the walk (Job 29:6) and the counterfeit appearing as smooth speech (Psalm 55:21)—map seamlessly onto this core definition without breaking the symbol.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The word worked from milk into sound judgment — teaching tested until one can refuse evil and choose good.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Worked into the walk, it cleanses the feet\",\n    \"Its counterfeit is smooth speech concealing war\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The connection between Jael serving butter in Judges 5:25 and the escalation of the word is an interesting application but relies heavily on external narrative assumptions.\",\n    \"Reading the provisions brought to David in 2 Samuel 17:29 as strict symbolic analogs may over-spiritualize historical logistics.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Butter/curds has no unified symbolic meaning; it is a literal dairy product whose metaphorical uses vary by context, serving as a figure of agricultural prosperity in Job, a pastoral diet during youth in Isaiah, and a simile for physical smoothness in Psalms.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A literal dairy product used in varied, context-dependent metaphors (wealth, pastoral diet, smoothness) rather than a unified symbol of doctrine.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"Butter does not systematically symbolize the worked word or sound judgment; its connection to discernment in Isaiah 7 simply marks the age of maturity attained while eating a common diet.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Isaiah 7:15 refers to eating a common pastoral diet until the child reaches the age of moral accountability, not a diet that induces or represents discernment itself.\",\n    \"Job 29:6 uses washing steps in butter as standard poetic hyperbole for immense agricultural wealth, paralleling rocks pouring oil.\",\n    \"Psalm 55:21 uses butter merely as a simile for physical smoothness to describe flattering speech.\",\n    \"Proverbs 30:33 uses churning butter as a practical, natural example of cause and effect, unrelated to spiritual teaching.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Unifies seemingly disparate passages into a single, cohesive definition rather than fracturing the word into four unrelated idioms.\",\n    \"Explains the specific juxtaposition in Isaiah 7:15 of this particular food with the cognitive outcome of discernment, aligning it structurally with the New Testament milk/solid food dichotomy.\",\n    \"Integrates the mechanical process of its creation (churning/working milk) with the established biblical metaphor of milk as the word.\",\n    \"Accounts for the specific use of 'words' being compared to butter in Psalm 55:21, keeping it within the same symbolic register.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Aligns effortlessly with ANE poetic idioms, such as Job 29:6 being a standard hyperbolic expression for material prosperity.\",\n    \"Does not require interpreting historical narrative details (like Jael or David's provisions) as coded spiritual allegory.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Isaiah 7:15\",\n    \"Proverbs 30:33\",\n    \"Psalm 55:21\",\n    \"Job 29:6\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"Job 29:6 is widely recognized as poetic hyperbole for immense material prosperity, and repurposing it to mean a spiritually cleansed walk relies on a rigid symbolic hermeneutic that overrides plain poetic reading.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
